  <section id="no-universal-rule">
    <h2>There is no universal standby-generator setback table</h2>
    <p>Placement depends on the exact generator enclosure and installation listing, doors/windows/air intakes, exhaust direction, combustible materials, service access, fuel equipment, property layout, local code, and the authority having jurisdiction (AHJ). A generic chart that says “all generators need X feet” can be dangerously incomplete.</p>
    <p>Get the <strong>current installation manual for the exact model and revision</strong> and ask the installer to confirm it with the local permit authority and utility before construction. Do not choose a pad location from this page or treat manufacturer examples below as permission for another model or site.</p>
    <p>The CPSC’s 2026 guidance says to operate <strong>portable generators outdoors at least 20 feet from homes and buildings</strong>, with exhaust directed away from openings. That is a portable-generator safety instruction; it is not a replacement for a permanently installed standby unit’s model-specific listed instructions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="manufacturer-examples">
    <h2>Two model-specific manufacturer examples</h2>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table min-w-[650px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Clearance wording in two exact standby-generator manufacturer documents</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Exact document</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">What the manufacturer states</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">What the statement does not establish</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Generac 22–28 kW G0073270 family specification sheet</th><td class="p-3">States listed/labeled installation as close as 18 inches to a structure for the models covered.</td><td class="p-3">The same sheet says to locate the unit away from doors, windows, and fresh-air intakes and follow local codes. It is not a universal setback or opening clearance.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">KOHLER/Rehlko G4-315 26RCA/26RCAL sheet</th><td class="p-3">States an aluminum enclosure can be installed as close as 18 inches from a home/small business; its footnote refers to clearance from combustible materials.</td><td class="p-3">The sheet says to check state/local code for minimum distance to a structure. It is not a general rule for all Kohler/other models or all site openings.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3">Both examples are limited to their referenced product documents. Use the complete installation manual to determine orientation, openings, airflow, access, fuel equipment, and all other site constraints.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="site-checklist">
    <h2>Site-assessment checklist for the installer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Exact generator model, serial/configuration, manual revision, and any applicable listed placement instructions.</li>
      <li>All building openings and air intakes; exhaust direction and potential re-entry paths.</li>
      <li>Clearance from combustible surfaces, walls, fences, landscaping, fuel equipment, meters, condensers, and property boundaries as applicable.</li>
      <li>Required service access, ventilation, overhead conditions, snow/vegetation management, and delivery/replacement access.</li>
      <li>Drainage, flood exposure, soil/base design, anchoring if required, and whether the proposed pad/base is manufacturer-approved.</li>
      <li>Local zoning, HOA, noise, fire/building, electrical/fuel permits, utility rules, and inspection approval.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ask the installer to identify the governing manual pages and have the local authority confirm any site-specific requirement. Keep the signed plans/permit documents with the equipment records.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="base-and-drainage">
    <h2>Base, drainage, and access</h2>
    <p>Pad dimensions, depth, material, level tolerance, anchors, gravel/concrete options, and drainage details are not universal. Use the exact installation manual and installer/engineer’s site plan. Do not pour a slab based on a generic “inches wider than the generator” rule.</p>
    <p>As one general planning reference, HomeGuide lists a $150–$400 range for a precast generator pad in its December 2025 guide. This is a published cost estimate, not a design specification or a guarantee that a particular base is permitted or adequate.</p>
    <p>Keep the planned service route accessible without blocking ventilation or creating a conflict with windows, doors, air intakes, meters, or neighboring property. A qualified installer must confirm final placement.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="noise-neighbors">
    <h2>Noise, neighbors, and property rules</h2>
    <p>Check local noise ordinances and HOA covenants before buying a unit or building an enclosure. Compare only model sound values with their stated distance, operating mode, load, and microphone direction; manufacturer ratings are not a prediction of sound at a property line. See our <a href="/brands/generac/">Generac</a> and <a href="/brands/kohler/">KOHLER</a> model pages for sourced conditions.</p>
    <p>Do not build a wall, planting screen, or cover that conflicts with airflow, exhaust, fire, access, or warranty instructions. Have a professional evaluate any noise mitigation plan before installation.</p>
  </section>
